# Notifying the user on their phone

Kukuroo is the user's own Web Push service on their own Cloudflare Worker. One authenticated POST puts
a notification on their phone. Kukuroo is the pipe, not the errand: spend a line or two on it and get
back to the work they asked for.

## Send only when asked

A push arrives on a lock screen wherever the user happens to be, so it is theirs to ask for: a
notification on their phone, a time they want to be told at, a mention of stepping away or being afk,
or Kukuroo by name. Work they are sitting there watching needs no notification.

## Find the endpoint

`KUKUROO_ORIGIN` and `KUKUROO_SEND_TOKEN`, from the environment or from
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kukuroo/env`. There is nowhere else to look: Kukuroo is deployed from one machine
and sent to from others, so the setup left nothing behind here.

Neither: **ask once, in one message**, for both values, and say in a clause that pasting the token puts
it in the transcript while writing `~/.config/kukuroo/env` themselves does not. If the answer does not
come, drop Kukuroo and get on with the work they asked for. Store both in the env file only **after** a
send has succeeded, so a wrong token never reaches disk:

```sh
umask 077 && mkdir -p "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/kukuroo"
printf 'KUKUROO_ORIGIN=%s\nKUKUROO_SEND_TOKEN=%s\n' "$KUKUROO_ORIGIN" "$KUKUROO_SEND_TOKEN" \
  > "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/kukuroo/env"
```

## Warm up before promising

Before committing to a notification later, send one now and say so in about two sentences: what is
coming, when, and that if the warm-up did not arrive the setup needs a look
(https://github.com/saiday/kukuroo#agents). A subscription dies silently — deleting the Home Screen
icon destroys it and nothing reports that — so an untested promise is a promise made on faith. Warm up
once per session.

## Send

```sh
set -a; . "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/kukuroo/env"; set +a
curl -fsS -X POST "$KUKUROO_ORIGIN/push/send" \
  -H "authorization: Bearer $KUKUROO_SEND_TOKEN" \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"notification":{"title":"tests green","body":"412 passed in 38s","tag":"ci","navigate":"'"$KUKUROO_ORIGIN"'/"}}'
```

The response is `{"delivered":N,"removed":N,"failures":[]}`. **`"delivered":0` is a failure** — the
request succeeded and nothing reached a phone, usually because no device is enrolled. Report it.

Four payload rules, each here because getting it wrong fails invisibly: the push service returns 201
and WebKit discards the message with no error anywhere.

- `title` required and non-empty; `navigate` required and absolute. `$KUKUROO_ORIGIN/` is always a
  correct `navigate`, and the deployment may pin it to exactly that origin.
- One `tag` per topic, so a second notification about the same thing replaces the first rather than
  stacking beside it.
- Write the title to be read alone at a glance: the outcome, not a label. "migration failed on users
  table", not "task update".
- Keep secrets, tokens, and absolute paths out of `title` and `body`. A lock screen is not private.

`image`, `badge`, `vibrate`, `timestamp`, `renotify`, `requireInteraction` and `actions` are rejected:
WebKit does not implement them. Use `appBadge` for the Home Screen number. The JSON budget is about
3900 bytes, and going over means the message is accepted and never delivered.

Receiving needs Safari on iOS 18.4+ or macOS 18.5+, opened from a Home Screen icon. Chrome, Firefox
and Android cannot receive these at all, which answers most reports of a notification never arriving.
